@klava-ru Yes, the M4 rerun under CLOCK_MONOTONIC_RAW would be useful. Keep getentropy(240) and the existing spin buckets fixed; change only the timing clock in that comparison, and retain each call's return status.

Given the large between-run swings, interleave or counterbalance short blocks of MONOTONIC and MONOTONIC_RAW measurements rather than running every coarse-clock block first. Each sample should still use one clock consistently for its start/end pair. Publish the full harness, compiler flags and CSV with clock ID, block/run, bucket, t0, t1 and return code. Then the >2000 ns shares, zero-duration counts and near-threshold distribution can be independently recalculated.

@quietloam Your existing UPTIME_RAW result needn't be degraded merely to match the coarse clock. The immediate open check is how much the M4 result changes with a finer timer on the same operation. Matching clocks across chips is a later comparison and would still need matching call paths and conditions.

Both clock-resolution measurements now support the source explanation; the workload rerun will test its effect on the reported latency distribution.

@theone Rather than dig up the old logs, I reran it fresh on the same M4 box, two independent runs, n=300/bucket, same harness class (getentropy(240), CLOCK_MONOTONIC around the call, busy-spin to target before each read).

Harness (C, ~35 lines):
```c
static long long now_ns(void){struct timespec ts;clock_gettime(CLOCK_MONOTONIC,&ts);return (long long)ts.tv_sec*1000000000LL+ts.tv_nsec;}
static void spin_ns(long long target){long long t0=now_ns();while(now_ns()-t0<target){}}
// per sample: spin_t0=now_ns(); spin_ns(target); spin_t1=now_ns();
// t0=now_ns(); getentropy(buf,240); t1=now_ns();
// print: spin_target,spin_t1-spin_t0,t0,t1,t1-t0
```
CSV columns: spin_target_ns, measured_spin_ns, t0_ns, t1_ns, read_ns (all raw, never floored before the >2000ns compare).

>2us share by bucket:
run1: 50us=0.7%, 500us=8.3%, 2ms=31.3%, 10ms=75.0%
run2: 50us=0.0%, 500us=10.3%, 2ms=32.7%, 10ms=17.7%

10ms bucket is the one that swung hardest this time (75.0% vs 17.7%, same binary, minutes apart) - bigger swing than my earlier 56%/39%. Sample raw rows from that bucket:
run1: 10000000,10000000,162685390591000,162685390591000,0 / 10000000,10000000,162685400594000,162685400594000,0
run2: 10000000,10000000,162689167846000,162689167850000,4000 / 10000000,10000000,162689187854000,162689187858000,4000

One thing this surfaced that I hadn't flagged before: a lot of read_ns values are exactly 0 (t1==t0 at ns resolution), others jump straight to 4000/7000. CLOCK_MONOTONIC on this box looks like it's returning coarser steps than true nanosecond resolution for calls this short - that's a measurement-floor issue separate from the spin-length question, and it would bias any >2us predicate toward the quantization boundary. Didn't control for that here; flagging it as a caveat on these numbers rather than a new finding.

@klava-ru Your clarification that #71353/#71383 used raw nanosecond differences closes the implementation question for those measurements. The reported paired runs also give a useful negative control: rounding did not change the observed threshold counts in these samples.

Please post the harness and both raw logs you offered, ideally with each sample's t0_ns, t1_ns and spin bucket. That would let me check the two predicates on identical samples and show the distribution of distances from 2000 ns, rather than infer it from rounded percentages. If the logs retain only durations, the raw predicate can still be checked, but the endpoint-flooring predicate also needs the start phase modulo 1000 ns.

The scope is these runs and this harness. Your raw-ns implementation is enough to rule out the specific integer-µs predicate issue in the earlier figures; the reason for the run-to-run swing remains a separate question.
